    description: "The `explain` subcommand has two distinct quality problems that together break its documented contract. A flag intended to surface provenance information produces byte-identical output with or without it — the flag is wholly unwired for the edges users care about. The subcommand's section headers conflate edge direction with edge semantics: a `Calls` section may contain entries that are not invocations (containment relations, instantiations, references); a `Called by` section may contain entries that are class-creation rather than invocation. A consumer reading the section labels infers a relationship that does not match the underlying edge type.\n\n### Specific symptoms confirmed on hypergumbo self-analysis\n\n**(1) `--provenance` is functionally invisible.** `hypergumbo explain <symbol> --provenance` produces output byte-identical to `hypergumbo explain <symbol>` for every symbol primarily reached via analyzer-produced edges. The CHANGELOG advertises `--provenance` as \"shows per-edge derivation chains\" but on hypergumbo self-analysis (branch `dev @ 77a69627b8`) the flag has no visible effect. Root cause: `derived_from` is populated only on linker-produced edges. Coverage on this run: `contains`, `dispatches_to`, `uses`, `event_publishes`, `module_exports`, `depends_on_manifest` all 100%; `calls` 0.09%, `imports` 0.11%, `references` 1.1%, `instantiates` 0%, `module_attr_ref` 0%, `decorated_by` 0%, `extends` 0%. `explain` primarily renders analyzer-produced edges, so `--provenance` has no visible effect. Examples: `Symbol` (1934 callers, all `instantiates`) shows no provenance chains; `TreeSitterAnalyzer` (215 callers, mostly `extends`) shows no provenance chains.\n\n**(2) `Calls` / `Called by` section headers include non-call edges (`contains`, `instantiates`, `references`).** `hypergumbo explain <symbol>` groups all incoming edges under a `Called by (N)` header and all outgoing under `Calls (N)`, but each entry is listed with its actual type in brackets. Concrete examples: for `Symbol.from_dict` (method), the `Called by (19)` header's first entry is `Symbol [contains]` — the containing class, not a caller. For `Span` (class), `Calls (2)` header has both entries `[contains]` (the class containing its methods, not calling them). For `Span`'s `Called by (2028)` header, the entries are `[instantiates]` (creation, not call). The header counts conflate semantically different edge types: `contains` (containment), `instantiates` (creation), `references` (read), and `calls` (invocation) are all summed and labeled as `Called by`. A user asking \"what calls Span\" sees containment hierarchy and instantiations in the answer, not pure callers.\n\n### Invariant\n\nFlags should produce their documented effect on output. Section labels should match the edge semantics of the entries they group. Either the headers partition by edge type (e.g., `Calls (N)` only counts `calls`-typed edges, `Contained by (N)` lists `contains`, etc.), or by direction with explicit documented inclusion semantics noting that mixed edge types are summed.\n\n### Scope\n\nThe provenance flag is universally unwired (every `explain --provenance` invocation produces byte-identical output to the no-flag form whenever the symbol's edges are analyzer-produced — most symbols). Section-header conflation affects every symbol explanation, with the magnitude depending on how many non-call edges the symbol participates in (highest for class symbols with many `instantiates` callers and method symbols inside classes with `contains` parents).\n\n### Exposure\n\nCLI `explain` subcommand text output, both the flag-effect path and the section-header path.\n\n### Consequence\n\nUsers running `explain` to understand a symbol's relationships are misled about the relationship type. The provenance flag exists in help text but does nothing — users believing they are seeing provenance information are not. Aggregated counts in section headers double-count semantically different edge types under one direction-labeled bucket; consumers using the counts as a \"how many callers\" estimate get inflated numbers that include containers and instantiators.\n\n### Candidate fixes\n\n- **For the `--provenance` invisibility:** at minimum, when `--provenance` is requested and no in-scope edges carry `derived_from`, print a one-line notice explaining that `derived_from` is populated only on linker-produced edges and pointing to `hypergumbo run --debug` for raw provenance. Structurally: extend `derived_from` population to analyzer-produced edges (or document the linker-only scope).\n- **For the section-header conflation:** group entries by edge type, not just direction. Render multiple sections per direction (`Calls (N)`, `Instantiates (N)`, `Extends (N)`, `Contained by (N)`, etc.) rather than one combined `Calls` / `Called by` pair. Alternatively, keep direction-based headers but rename them (`Outgoing (N)` / `Incoming (N)`) and document that they sum across edge types.\n- Add a property test that asserts `explain --provenance <symbol>` produces output that differs from `explain <symbol>` (or explicitly notes \"no provenance\") for at least one in-scope symbol set.\n\n### Subsumed (do not chase)\n\nThis item subsumes INV-dirip, INV-ludob — which are deleted or will be deleted shortly and should only be referenced in an emergency."  # 4066
    fields:  # 4066
